{"answer": "It depends on the release. Before the HAL Engine release that stops calling `delete` on socket close (hal-engine-session-lifetime #117, the release named in FR-011), `store.delete(sessionId)` only evicted the in-memory cache entry. It returned true but left the MongoDB document in place, so `repository.load(sessionId)` still returned the conversation (FR-066, FR-012). Real GDPR erasure went through the separate `eraseConversation` repository method.

From that release on, per issue #152 (closed) and FR-013 to FR-017, `delete` is a composite erase. It erases the stored document first, then evicts the cache entry. It returns `Promise<boolean>`: true if a document or cache entry was removed, false if neither existed. Afterwards both `repository.load(sessionId)` and `store.get(sessionId)` are undefined. If the document erase rejects, the cache entry stays and the rejection propagates, so a partial erasure is never reported as done.

I found the spec and the issue but did not read the code. I can't confirm from here that the #152 behavior is what the repo currently implements.", "sources": ["specs/support-agent-conversation-data/spec.md", "issue-152", "issue-591"]}

